ologise later.\\n\\nBoth create friction. Both land on you.\\n\\nHere's what I want to ask you honestly: is the problem showing up in one team, or is it showing up everywhere at once?\\n\\nThat distinction matters more than most people realise.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply with your honest answer — one team or everywhere?\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The answer to that question changes what the fix looks like. In Email 4, I'll walk you through the framework we use to figure out which problem you actually have.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 8,\n    \"subject\": \"The thing two consultants missed\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Sarah Chen said it plainly: 'The diagnostic alone surfaced four issues neither had touched.'\",\n    \"body\": \"I get asked all the time: what makes this different from the last consultant they hired?\\n\\nThe honest answer is that most org design work starts with structure. We start with decisions.\\n\\nHere's the principle: you can't design a structure that distributes authority clearly until you've mapped which decisions actually drive the business — and who is currently making them versus who is supposed to.\\n\\nMost organisations have never done that mapping explicitly. They've inherited it.\\n\\nThe proof is in the diagnostic phase. Sarah Chen, a Chief of Staff who'd worked with two prior consultants, put it directly: \"The diagnostic alone surfaced four issues neither had touched.\"\\n\\nThat's not because the other consultants were bad. It's because they started in the wrong place.\\n\\nThe framework has three phases: map the real decision landscape, identify where authority and accountability are misaligned, then build a decision-rights structure designed for where the company is going — not where it's been.\\n\\nSimple in principle. Specific in practice.\\n\\nWant me to walk you through how it applies to your current situation?\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply and tell me your biggest decision-rights blind spot right now\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — In the next email, I'll show you what this looks like in practice — a specific before-and-after that you'll probably recognise.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 12,\n    \"subject\": \"Three months. Leadership tension down.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Tom Aldridge described what held after the reorg. It's worth reading exactly how he said it.\",\n    \"body\": \"Tom Aldridge is a COO at a fast-scaling SaaS company. He came in with a problem most people in his role recognise immediately.\\n\\nWeekly leadership meetings full of tension that nobody could quite explain or resolve. A reorg that had been attempted and had quietly un-reorged itself within a quarter.\\n\\nThe root cause, once mapped, was a mismatch between who was accountable for outcomes and who had authority to make the decisions that drove those outcomes. They weren't the same people.\\n\\nSo every major call triggered a negotiation nobody had agreed to have.\\n\\nThe work wasn't about moving boxes on a chart. It was about explicitly assigning decision rights to match accountability — and making that legible to the whole leadership team.\\n\\nThree months later, Tom said it plainly: \"Three months later our weekly leadership tension is structurally lower. The reorg held through ou...\"\\n\\nThe reorg held. That's the part that matters.\\n\\nNot because the people changed. Because the architecture did.\\n\\nDoes the pattern Tom started with sound familiar to you?\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply and tell me if Tom's starting point sounds like yours\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — In my next email I'm going to address the two things people usually try before working with us — and why they don't stick. If you've already been down one of those roads, that email is for you.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 16,\n    \"subject\": \"You've probably already tried one of these\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Neither is wrong. Both usually fail to hold past the next hiring push. Here's the structural reason why.\",\n    \"body\": \"When org friction gets bad enough, most COOs try one of two things.\\n\\nThey bring in a consultant to redesign the org chart. Or they run a leadership offsite to realign on roles and accountabilities.\\n\\nNeither is a bad instinct. Both tend to fail inside six months.\\n\\nHere's why. The org chart is a reporting structure. It tells you who manages whom. It doesn't tell you who decides what — and that's the question that generates the friction.\\n\\nThe offsite gets everyone aligned in a room. But alignment without a documented, operating decision-rights structure dissolves the moment the next ambiguous situation hits. Which is usually within weeks.\\n\\nThe most common objection I hear at this point is: \"We've already tried something like this and it didn't stick.\"\\n\\nThat's almost always true. And it didn't stick because the intervention addressed the visible symptom — confusion, tension, escalation — rather than the underlying architecture.\\n\\nA decision-rights structure built on explicit mapping holds through growth because it's designed around the decisions, not the people currently making them.\\n\\nStill on the fence? What's the question?\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply with the question that's still sitting on the fence for you\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — My next email is the one where I lay out exactly what working together looks like — and what it's produced. If you've been reading along, that email is worth opening.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 19,\n    \"subject\": \"Here's what I'm offering and who it's for\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Limited cohort. One clear next step. Here's everything you need to decide.\",\n    \"body\": \"If you've read this far, you already know the problem is architectural — not a people problem, not a communication problem.\\n\\nHere's what I'm offering.\\n\\nA [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION] engagement — 1:1, built around your org's actual decision landscape. Not a template. Not a framework you apply yourself after a workshop.\\n\\nBy the end, you leave with a documented decision-rights structure that your leadership team can operate from — and that holds through the next year of growth.\\n\\nTom Aldridge saw structural reduction in weekly leadership tension within three months. Sarah Chen's diagnostic surfaced four issues two prior consultants hadn't reached.\\n\\nHere's what you get before we even begin: a diagnostic that maps your real decision landscape and shows you exactly where authority and accountability are misaligned. Most clients say that alone is worth the engagement.\\n\\nGuarantee: [INSERT_GUARANTEE_TERMS].\\n\\nThis cohort is limited. It starts [INSERT_PROGRAMME_START_DATE].\\n\\nIf this is the right moment to fix the architecture — not patch it — the next step is a conversation.\\n\\nEnrol now and claim your place before the cohort fills.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now and claim your place in the next cohort\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — If you're not sure this is the right fit, book the call anyway. The diagnostic conversation alone will show you something about your org that you haven't been able to name yet.\"\n  }\n]"}
