[emailSequences] RETRY EXHAUST — FULL RAW CONTENT (from invokeLLM, post tool-use stringify): {"emails":"[\n  {\n    \"day\": -7,\n    \"subject\": \"something's been keeping me up\",\n    \"previewText\": \"It's not the usual stuff. It's something I've been quietly building — and it's almost ready.\",\n    \"body\": \"The conversation that breaks something open usually isn't the big one.\\n\\nIt's not the board presentation. It's not the all-hands. It's a Tuesday afternoon when someone asks who owns this decision — and nobody can answer.\\n\\nI've been watching that moment happen inside fast-growing companies for a long time.\\n\\nAnd for the last [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION] I've been building something specifically for the COOs and Chiefs of Staff who are living inside that moment right now.\\n\\nNot a framework. Not a slide deck. A structured process that resolves it.\\n\\nI'm not ready to share everything yet.\\n\\nBut I wanted you to know it's coming — because if you've ever sat in a room where nobody could agree on who decides what, this is going to be relevant to you.\\n\\nWatch for my next email in a few days.\\n\\nThere's a principle inside this work that I think will change how you see the whole problem.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Watch for the next email — it arrives in a few days.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The COOs I built this with described the core problem in a specific way. I'll share exactly how they phrased it in the next email. It might sound familiar.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": -4,\n    \"subject\": \"the reorg didn't fail. the design did.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Most org fixes treat the symptom. Here's what's actually underneath the friction — and why it keeps coming back.\",\n    \"body\": \"Here's the belief that's costing fast-growing companies the most right now: that org friction is a people problem.\\n\\nIt's not. It's an architecture problem.\\n\\nWhen your company crosses 80, 100, 120 people, the informal decision-making layer that carried you breaks. Nobody is doing anything wrong. The structure itself can no longer hold the weight.\\n\\nMost companies respond by reorging. They move boxes around the chart.\\n\\nBut the chart was never where decisions actually lived.\\n\\nDecisions live in understood — and mostly unspoken — agreements about who has authority over what. When those agreements stop being shared, you get reorg paralysis, role ambiguity, and a leadership team that's technically aligned but practically stuck.\\n\\nThe fix isn't another restructure. The fix is making the decision-rights layer legible.\\n\\nWhen you can see exactly who owns what — and the whole leadership team agrees — the friction drops. Not gradually. Structurally.\\n\\nThat's the principle behind everything I've been building.\\n\\nAnd in a few days, I'm going to show you exactly what that looks like in practice.\\n\\nDoes this match what you're experiencing right now?\\n\\nHit reply and tell me. I read every response.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Hit reply — does this match what you're seeing inside your org right now?\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — In the next email, I'll show you what happens when a leadership team applies this principle for the first time. The shift is faster than most people expect.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": -2,\n    \"subject\": \"what four issues looked like before we found them\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Two prior consultants missed them entirely. Here's what the diagnostic actually surfaces — and why it's different.\",\n    \"body\": \"Here's what the diagnostic phase of this process actually does.\\n\\nIt doesn't start with an org chart. It doesn't start with a survey or a leadership offsite.\\n\\nIt starts by mapping where decisions are actually being made — not where the chart says they should be made.\\n\\nIn most companies past 100 people, those two maps don't match. The gap between them is where all the friction lives: the reorg paralysis, the \"who owns this?\" conversations that never resolve, the promotion-but-no-clarity situations where someone gets a new title and the same unclear scope.\\n\\nWhen you make that gap visible, you can start closing it.\\n\\nSarah Chen, a Chief of Staff, described it this way after going through the diagnostic:\\n\\n\"We had tried two prior consultants. The diagnostic alone surfaced four issues neither had touched.\"\\n\\nThat's what specificity does. It finds what generalist approaches miss.\\n\\nThe process I've built runs this diagnostic first — before any recommendations, before any restructuring conversation. Because the right fix depends entirely on what the diagnostic finds.\\n\\nI'm sharing all of it — the full process, the structure, the outcomes — when the doors open on [INSERT_CART_OPEN_DATE].\\n\\nThat's 48 hours from now.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Doors open [INSERT_CART_OPEN_DATE] — watch for that email.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Tom Aldridge, a COO who went through the process, said something specific about what changed three months later. I'll share his exact words when the cart opens.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 0,\n    \"subject\": \"it's live.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"The programme is open. Here's everything that's included — and what happens from the moment you enrol.\",\n    \"body\": \"It's here. [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] is now open for enrolment.\\n\\nHere's exactly what you get.\\n\\nA [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION] structured engagement working directly with [INSERT_HOST_NAME] — built for COOs and Chiefs of Staff whose companies have grown past the point where informal decision-making still works.\\n\\nThe process has three phases: a forensic diagnostic that maps where decisions are actually being made, a decision-rights design session that builds a clear, agreed authority structure across your leadership team, and an implementation track that stress-tests that structure against your next quarter of real decisions.\\n\\nThe outcome: decision-rights clarity that survives the next year of growth — not a framework that looks good in a deck and collapses under pressure.\\n\\nTom Aldridge, a COO who completed the process, said it directly: \"Three months later our weekly leadership tension is structurally lower. The reorg held through ou—\"\\n\\nThat's real. That's the result.\\n\\nThe investment is [INSERT_PRICE].\\n\\nThe guarantee: [INSERT_GUARANTEE_TERMS].\\n\\nEnrolment is limited to [INSERT_COHORT_LIMIT] organisations per cohort. When those places are filled, the cart closes.\\n\\nCart closes [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE] at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\\n\\nIf your company is stuck in the \"who decides what\" loop right now, this is the process that resolves it.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now and claim your place in this cohort.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The diagnostic alone has surfaced issues that two prior consultants missed entirely. That phase starts in your first week.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 1,\n    \"subject\": \"specifically for the COO who's already tried the reorg\",\n    \"previewText\": \"You moved the boxes. The friction didn't move. Here's why — and what actually fixes it.\",\n    \"body\": \"If you're a COO who has already run a reorg in the last 18 months — and the org friction came back — this email is specifically for you.\\n\\nYou did the work. You consulted the leadership team. You redesigned the chart.\\n\\nAnd six months later the same \"who owns this?\" conversations are back. The same decisions are stalling. The same people are stepping on each other's lanes.\\n\\nHere's what happened: the reorg fixed the visible structure. It didn't fix the decision-rights layer underneath it.\\n\\nThat layer is where authority actually lives. It's the unspoken agreements — about who can move without asking, who needs to sign off, who owns the outcome — that your leadership team accumulated informally when you were smaller.\\n\\nWhen the company scales past those agreements, the reorg paralysis starts. And rearranging the chart doesn't touch it.\\n\\n[INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] starts exactly there. The diagnostic maps the real decision-rights layer — not the chart, not the RACI — and makes it legible.\\n\\nThen we redesign it deliberately. With your full leadership team aligned.\\n\\nThe result isn't a new org chart. It's a shared operating system that holds through the next phase of growth.\\n\\nEnrolment is open now. Cart closes [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE] at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now and start with the diagnostic in your first week.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The cohort is limited to [INSERT_COHORT_LIMIT] organisations. If you're reading this, there are still places available — but that changes as enrolments come in.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 3,\n    \"subject\": \"you're probably thinking this one thing\",\n    \"previewText\": \"It's the most common reason people don't move forward. Let's look at it directly.\",\n    \"body\": \"Here's the objection sitting in your head right now: \"This sounds right, but my situation is too specific for an outside process to actually work.\"\\n\\nI hear this from almost every COO and Chief of Staff before they start.\\n\\nYour company's friction feels uniquely shaped. Your leadership team has history. Your founders have strong opinions. Your context is complicated.\\n\\nThat's exactly why this process starts with a diagnostic rather than a recommendation.\\n\\nEvery engagement begins by mapping your specific decision-rights layer — not applying a generic framework over the top of your org. The output of the diagnostic is a picture of your company, not a template.\\n\\nSarah Chen said it directly after the diagnostic phase: \"We had tried two prior consultants. The diagnostic alone surfaced four issues neither had touched.\"\\n\\nThose weren't generic issues. They were specific to her organisation. That's the point.\\n\\nNow — is this a fit for everyone? No.\\n\\nIf your leadership team isn't willing to engage honestly with the diagnostic findings, the process won't hold. This works for COOs and Chiefs of Staff who want a structural solution, not validation for a decision they've already made.\\n\\nIf that's you, this is built for you.\\n\\nEnrolment is open now through [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE] at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now and bring your specific situation into the diagnostic.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — In two days I'm adding something to the enrolment package I haven't mentioned yet. It has a specific dollar value. Watch for that email.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 5,\n    \"subject\": \"I almost forgot to mention this.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"There's one more thing inside the enrolment package. I should have led with this.\",\n    \"body\": \"I've been so focused on the core process that I almost didn't mention this.\\n\\nEveryone who enrols in [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] before [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE] also receives a bonus I'm calling the Decision-Rights Rapid Audit.\\n\\nThis is a standalone diagnostic tool — valued at [INSERT_BONUS_VALUE] — that you can run with your leadership team in under two hours.\\n\\nIt surfaces the top three decision-rights conflicts inside your current structure: the places where two or more people believe they have authority over the same outcome.\\n\\nMost leadership teams don't know those conflicts exist until a high-stakes moment exposes them. This audit finds them before that happens.\\n\\nYou can use it before the main programme begins. Or run it independently with any team.\\n\\nIt's not a worksheet. It's a structured facilitation guide with scoring criteria that make the conflicts legible — not just felt.\\n\\nThis is only available to people who enrol before the cart closes at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME] on [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE].\\n\\nAfter that, it's not included.\\n\\nThe full package — [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION] programme plus the Rapid Audit — is [INSERT_PRICE].\\n\\nThat's the complete offer.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now and claim the Decision-Rights Rapid Audit before the cart closes.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The guarantee is [INSERT_GUARANTEE_TERMS]. If you've been sitting on this, that removes the remaining risk.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 7,\n    \"subject\": \"closes tonight.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Cart closes at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME] on [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE]. Here's what disappears when it does.\",\n    \"body\": \"The cart for [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] closes tonight at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\\n\\nAfter that, three things are gone.\\n\\nFirst: enrolment in this cohort. The cohort is limited to [INSERT_COHORT_LIMIT] organisations. When the cart closes, the remaining places close with it. The next cohort opens [INSERT_COHORT_CLOSE_DATE].\\n\\nSecond: the Decision-Rights Rapid Audit bonus. Valued at [INSERT_BONUS_VALUE]. Not available after tonight.\\n\\nThird: access to [INSERT_HOST_NAME] at this intensity level. The [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION] engagement is a direct working relationship — not a course, not a content library. That level of access is what produces results like Tom Aldridge's: \"Three months later our weekly leadership tension is structurally lower. The reorg held through ou—\"\\n\\nIf your company is still in the \"nobody can agree on who decides what\" loop right now, tonight is the decision point.\\n\\nThe investment is [INSERT_PRICE].\\n\\nThe guarantee is [INSERT_GUARANTEE_TERMS].\\n\\nThe cart closes at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\\n\\nEnrol before then.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now before the cart closes at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — I'm sending one final email later today with a specific answer to the question I've seen most in replies this week. That email will be the last one I send about this programme.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 7,\n    \"subject\": \"last email about this.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"You've been asking one question all week. Here's the honest answer — before tonight's deadline.\",\n    \"body\": \"You've been wondering: \"What if I do this and my leadership team doesn't buy in?\"\\n\\nHere's the honest answer.\\n\\nThe process is designed to produce buy-in as an output — not assume it as an input. The diagnostic phase puts objective data in front of your leadership team. It shows them the decision-rights conflicts in neutral language, not in the language of whoever has the strongest opinion.\\n\\nWhen leaders see the actual gap between \"who the chart says owns this\" and \"who actually makes this call,\" the conversation changes. It becomes structural, not political.\\n\\nThat's why Tom Aldridge's reorg held. Not because his team suddenly agreed on everything — but because the decision-rights layer was made legible first.\\n\\nSo here's the binary choice you're sitting with tonight.\\n\\nYou enrol before [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME], go through the diagnostic, and leave with a decision-rights structure your whole leadership team agreed to build together.\\n\\nOr you don't, the current friction continues, and the next reorg looks like the last one.\\n\\nCart closes at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME] on [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE].\\n\\nThis is the last email I'll send about [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME].\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now — the cart closes at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME] tonight.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The guarantee is [INSERT_GUARANTEE_TERMS]. The decision to try this carries less risk than another quarter of reorg paralysis.\"\n  }\n]"}
