[emailSequences] RETRY EXHAUST — FULL RAW CONTENT (from invokeLLM, post tool-use stringify): {"emails":"[\n  {\n    \"day\": 0,\n    \"subject\": \"it's inside (took 11 seconds to send)\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Your copy of [INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME] is here — plus something I want to show you over the next few weeks.\",\n    \"body\": \"[INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME] is attached — open it when you have 20 minutes and a quiet tab.\\n\\nI'm [INSERT_HOST_NAME], and I help ecommerce and SaaS founders turn their four most-sent emails into their hardest-working ones.\\n\\nOver the next three weeks I'm going to share the stuff that didn't fit in the download. The patterns I keep seeing in inboxes. The one shift that moves open rates faster than any subject-line hack. And the reason most founders fix the wrong email first.\\n\\nYou don't need to do anything except read what lands.\\n\\nBut before Email 2 arrives — try this: open your last broadcast email and read only the first sentence.\\n\\nJust that one sentence.\\n\\nDoes it make you want to read the second one? Or does it just… explain what the email is about?\\n\\nSit with that. I'll come back to it in a couple of days.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Download [INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME]\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Email 2 is coming in 48 hours. It's about the thing most founders fix last — but should fix first. Worth waiting for.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 2,\n    \"subject\": \"the email nobody rewrites first\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Everyone goes for the subject line. The real lever is somewhere else — and it's already in your account.\",\n    \"body\": \"I've noticed something specific about founders who get under 15% open rates.\\n\\nThey always start with subject lines.\\n\\nThey A/B test, they add emojis, they copy formulas from Twitter threads — and their open rates nudge up maybe two points and then flatline.\\n\\nHere's what's actually happening: a weak subject line is almost never the root problem.\\n\\nThe root problem is your welcome email.\\n\\nYour welcome email sets every expectation your subscriber will ever have about your emails. If it sounds like an autoresponder wrote it, they've already decided not to read you — before your next campaign even goes out.\\n\\nMost founders spend 40 minutes on a campaign and four minutes on their welcome sequence.\\n\\nThat's backwards.\\n\\nFix the welcome email and your whole list recalibrates. Open rates on every subsequent email lift — because the relationship started differently.\\n\\nThis is the one I'd look at before anything else in [INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME].\\n\\nReply and tell me: when did you last reread your own welcome email? I'm curious whether what I'm describing fits your situation.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply and tell me when you last read your welcome email\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — In a few days I'm going to name the exact moment in your week where low open rates are actually created. It's not when you hit send.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 5,\n    \"subject\": \"the moment your open rate dies\",\n    \"previewText\": \"It's not the send time. It's not the list size. It happens much earlier — and you probably don't notice it.\",\n    \"body\": \"Here's where it actually happens.\\n\\nYou've finished a product update, a sale, or a new post. You need to email your list. You open your email tool, stare at the blank subject line field, and type something that describes what you're about to say.\\n\\n\"New feature: [X] is live.\" \"Our sale ends Sunday.\" \"Here's this week's update.\"\\n\\nYou hit send. You check open rates the next morning. Under 15% again.\\n\\nThe email didn't die when you hit send. It died the moment you wrote a subject line that told them what was inside instead of making them need to find out.\\n\\nDescriptive subject lines are the single most common pattern in low-performing inboxes.\\n\\nBut here's what makes it stick: nobody taught you to write curiosity-first. Every system notification, every work email, every Shopify alert you've ever received was descriptive. You learned by example — the wrong example.\\n\\nThis isn't a skill gap. It's a habit installed by years of reading corporate email.\\n\\nDoes that land for you? Does \"I write subject lines that describe what's inside\" sound like what you're doing right now?\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply yes or no — does this describe your subject lines right now?\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Next email I'll show you the four-word test I use to check any subject line before it goes out. Takes less time than re-reading the email.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 8,\n    \"subject\": \"why your emails sound like a robot\",\n    \"previewText\": \"It's not your tone. It's not your word choice. There's one structural reason — and it's fixable in an afternoon.\",\n    \"body\": \"I get asked this constantly: why do my emails feel so stiff even when I'm trying to be natural?\\n\\nThe answer almost always comes down to one thing: you're writing one email that's trying to do three jobs.\\n\\nHere's the principle that changes everything — one email, one job.\\n\\nEvery time an email tries to announce something, sell something, and nurture the relationship at once, it fails at all three. The reader senses the confusion. It reads like a robot because no single human thought works that way.\\n\\nWhen I started applying this to the four core email types — welcome, abandoned cart, post-purchase, re-engagement — open rates and click-throughs both moved. Not because the words got better. Because each email finally had a clear reason to exist.\\n\\nThe four templates in [INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME] are each built on this principle. One job per email, executed cleanly.\\n\\nWant me to walk you through how it applies specifically to your abandoned cart sequence?\\n\\nThat's usually the one where founders are trying hardest to do too much — and leaving the most revenue on the table.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply and I'll walk you through the abandoned cart application\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The next email I send is about what actually changes when a founder rewrites these four emails. The structural shift, not just the metrics.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 12,\n    \"subject\": \"what changes when the email finally works\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Not the open rate number — something that happens before that. A structural shift that makes every email easier to write after.\",\n    \"body\": \"Here's what the one-email-one-job method actually produces — and it's not just a metrics story.\\n\\nWhen a founder builds their four core emails around a single clear job each, something structural shifts.\\n\\nThey stop dreading their send days.\\n\\nBefore the rewrite, sitting down to write an email means managing conflict — the email has to sell, inform, and build trust all at once, and those goals pull in different directions. The email sounds like a robot because it's trying to serve three masters.\\n\\nAfter the rewrite, there's a decision already made before the cursor hits the blank page: this email has one job. The words come faster. The tone comes through.\\n\\nThe open rate lifts — but the first thing a founder notices is that the email took half the time to write and they actually want to send it.\\n\\nDo you recognise the starting-point pattern here? The dread before the blank page, the email that ends up doing too much, the open rate that confirms what you already suspected?\\n\\nIf that's your Monday morning right now, the method in [INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME] was built specifically for that moment.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Download [INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME] and apply the method this week\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Email 6 is coming soon. It covers the thing founders usually try before this — and why it almost always makes the problem worse instead of better.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 16,\n    \"subject\": \"the fix that made your open rates worse\",\n    \"previewText\": \"You probably tried it. It probably worked for about a week. Here's what was actually happening under the surface.\",\n    \"body\": \"The obvious move when your open rates are low is to fix your subject lines.\\n\\nSo you find a swipe file, pick a formula, test a few variations. Open rates tick up for a week. Then they settle back.\\n\\nHere's what happened: you borrowed someone else's curiosity without changing what the email actually delivers. Subscribers opened — and found the same email that's been disappointing them for months.\\n\\nA subject line is a promise. If the email doesn't pay it off, the next subject line gets ignored — because they already know what's inside.\\n\\nThat's why subject-line fixes are temporary. The welcome email, the post-purchase sequence, the re-engagement flow — those are where trust is actually built or lost.\\n\\nNow — the objection I hear most: \"I don't have time to rewrite four email sequences from scratch.\"\\n\\nYou're not rewriting from scratch. [INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME] gives you the templates. You're filling in your product, your voice, your offer. Most founders finish all four in a weekend.\\n\\nStill on the fence? What's the question — reply and I'll answer it directly.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply with your question and I'll answer it directly\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Next email is the last one in this sequence. I'll be making a specific offer — and it's only available to this list for a limited time.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 19,\n    \"subject\": \"this is what I'm offering you\",\n    \"previewText\": \"No more build-up. Here's exactly what's available, what it includes, and what happens when you say yes.\",\n    \"body\": \"You've been getting under 15% open rates. Your emails sound stiff. Writing them takes longer than it should.\\n\\nYou downloaded [INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME] because you wanted a faster way through.\\n\\nHere's what I want to offer you next.\\n\\nThe eBook and workbook bundle gives you the four highest-converting email types — welcome, abandoned cart, post-purchase, and re-engagement — with done-for-you templates and the structural logic behind each one.\\n\\nYou can rewrite all four this weekend. Not in theory. With the templates open and your product details in hand.\\n\\nWhen these four emails are working, your list is earning for you between campaigns — not just on send days.\\n\\nHere's what's included: the full eBook with the one-email-one-job framework applied to all four types, the workbook with fill-in templates for each, and the four-word subject line test from Email 3.\\n\\nThe guarantee: [INSERT_GUARANTEE_TERMS].\\n\\nThe programme begins: [INSERT_PROGRAMME_START_DATE].\\n\\nThis offer is available to this list for a limited enrolment window — cohort size is capped and places fill from this list first.\\n\\nClick below to enrol now.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now and claim your place\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — If you're still unsure whether this is right for your situation, reply with one question. I read every reply and I'll give you a straight answer.\"\n  }\n]"}
