[emailSequences] RETRY EXHAUST — FULL RAW CONTENT (from invokeLLM, post tool-use stringify): {"emails":"[\n  {\n    \"day\": -7,\n    \"subject\": \"I almost deleted this draft\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Six months watching ecommerce founders send emails nobody opens — I finally did something about it.\",\n    \"body\": \"Your open rate is telling you something your gut already knows.\\n\\nSomething's off. The emails go out. The silence comes back.\\n\\nI've spent the last several months obsessing over one specific problem: founders who are brilliant at their product and completely invisible in their inbox.\\n\\nNot because they don't care. Because nobody showed them the part that actually moves the needle.\\n\\nI've been building something to fix that. Something specific, fast, and built for people who don't have a week to read an email marketing textbook.\\n\\nI'm not ready to share everything yet.\\n\\nBut I wanted you to know it's coming — because when the doors open, they won't stay open long.\\n\\nWatch for my next email in a few days. I'll share the one principle that changed how I think about every email I write.\\n\\nIt's counterintuitive. It might annoy you a little. Good.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Watch for the next email — it lands in a few days.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — If your last campaign got under 15% open rates, this is specifically for you. Not generally. Specifically.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": -4,\n    \"subject\": \"Your subject line isn't the problem\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Every email course tells you to fix your subject line first. That's exactly backwards — here's why.\",\n    \"body\": \"Every email course tells you to fix your subject line first.\\n\\nThat's exactly backwards.\\n\\nHere's what's actually happening: your reader opens the email, reads the first sentence, and leaves. The subject line got them in. The first sentence lost them.\\n\\nMost founders write their first sentence like a news anchor. Context first, then the point. \"I'm reaching out today because...\" Nobody cares.\\n\\nThe readers who stick — and click — arrive because the first sentence already named something true about their life.\\n\\nNot clever. Not formal. True.\\n\\nWhen your email opens with a sentence that makes someone think \"how did they know that?\" — everything after it gets read differently.\\n\\nThat's the principle. It sounds simple. It isn't easy. Most founders have been trained by corporate email culture to bury the lead, hedge everything, and sound professional.\\n\\nProfessional doesn't convert. Specific and true converts.\\n\\nI'm building something around this exact principle — more on that very soon.\\n\\nBut first: does this match what you're seeing in your own emails? Hit reply and tell me. I read every response.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Hit reply and tell me what your first sentences usually look like.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The next email I send will show you what this looks like in practice. And it'll include something I haven't told anyone yet.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": -2,\n    \"subject\": \"What happens at sentence one\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Here's the mechanism nobody explains — and why it's the reason your open rates are stuck right where they are.\",\n    \"body\": \"Here's what the method actually does — and why it works structurally.\\n\\nMost email frameworks teach you to optimise for the open. Subject line testing, send-time testing, list segmentation. All of it is trying to get the click on the envelope.\\n\\nNone of it addresses what happens after.\\n\\nThe method behind [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] works differently. It starts at sentence one of the body and works outward from there.\\n\\nHere's the structure: every email has one job. Not two. One. And sentence one names the exact situation the reader is in right now — using the words they'd use to describe it to a friend, not the words a marketer would use.\\n\\nWhen that's right, the rest of the email gets read with a completely different posture. The reader isn't scanning. They're checking to see if you keep being right.\\n\\nThat's the shift. From filtering to listening.\\n\\nA founder managing their own email list — someone wearing every hat, writing campaigns between Slack messages and fulfilment issues — can apply this to their four most-sent emails in a weekend. No theory. No 12-module course. Just a rewrite with the right template in front of them.\\n\\nThat's exactly what's coming.\\n\\nCart opens [INSERT_CART_OPEN_DATE]. I'll send you everything then.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Mark [INSERT_CART_OPEN_DATE] — that's when the doors open.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — There's a fast-action bonus I'm including for the first people through the door. I'll explain it in the next email.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 0,\n    \"subject\": \"It's live.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Everything you need to rewrite your four most-sent emails this weekend — no theory, no fluff, no waiting.\",\n    \"body\": \"[INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] is open.\\n\\nHere's exactly what you're getting:\\n\\nFour fill-in-the-blank email templates — welcome, abandoned cart, post-purchase, and re-engagement — rebuilt from the ground up using the sentence-one-first method I've been walking you through.\\n\\nEach template includes the line-by-line logic so you understand why every sentence is where it is. You're not just copying — you're learning the pattern you'll use on every email after this.\\n\\nA 20-minute audio walkthrough showing you how to adapt each template to your specific product and voice. Fast. No fluff.\\n\\nA subject line swipe file — 40 curiosity-driven subject lines sorted by email type, ready to use or model.\\n\\nTotal price: [INSERT_PRICE].\\n\\nGuarantee: [INSERT_GUARANTEE_TERMS].\\n\\nThis isn't a course you'll watch someday. It's a weekend rewrite you'll finish before Monday. Founders with under 15% open rates have used this exact framework to get their emails actually read — without hiring a copywriter or learning email theory.\\n\\nYou can keep sending emails that sound like a robot wrote them.\\n\\nOr you can fix the four that matter most, this weekend.\\n\\nThe cart closes [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE] at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\",\n    \"cta\": \"Get [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] now — [INSERT_PRICE].\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — There's a fast-action bonus for the first [INSERT_COHORT_LIMIT] buyers. It's not listed on the sales page. Check your next email.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 1,\n    \"subject\": \"If you're too busy to fix this\",\n    \"previewText\": \"The founder who's running everything solo has a specific version of this problem. This email is for them.\",\n    \"body\": \"If you're a solo founder managing your own email list between everything else — this one's specifically for you.\\n\\nYou didn't get here because you're bad at marketing. You got here because email was never your primary job.\\n\\nYou set up a welcome sequence at 11pm six months ago. It's still running. You haven't looked at it since. Your open rates are sitting below 15% and you know it, but every time you sit down to fix it, you don't know where to start — so you don't.\\n\\nThat's not a motivation problem. That's a template problem.\\n\\nWhen you don't have a structure to drop your words into, \"fix the emails\" becomes a project. Projects get deferred. Templates get done.\\n\\n[INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] gives you four templates for the exact emails eating your open rates right now. Welcome. Abandoned cart. Post-purchase. Re-engagement.\\n\\nYou rewrite them this weekend using the fill-in-the-blank format. You're done by Sunday.\\n\\nNo agency. No course. No six-week plan.\\n\\nJust four emails that stop sounding like a robot — written in your voice, in a weekend, for [INSERT_PRICE].\",\n    \"cta\": \"Get the templates and rewrite your emails this weekend.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The cart closes [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE] at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME]. If this weekend doesn't work, next weekend will be too