[emailSequences] RETRY EXHAUST — FULL RAW CONTENT (from invokeLLM, post tool-use stringify): {"emails":"[\n  {\n    \"day\": 1,\n    \"subject\": \"you're ghosted after the proposal.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Not because of your price. Because of something that happened three steps earlier — your resource is inside.\",\n    \"body\": \"Most founders who lose £50k deals don't lose them on price.\\n\\nThey lose them in the discovery call — before the proposal even exists.\\n\\nHere's [INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME], exactly what you signed up for.\\n\\n[LINK TO INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME]\\n\\nOne thing shifts for founders who engage with material like this: they stop treating the discovery call as information-gathering.\\n\\nThey start treating it as the sale itself.\\n\\nThat reframe changes everything — what you ask, when you go quiet, how you handle \"we need to think about it.\"\\n\\nOpen it now. It's short. It's specific.\\n\\nIt was built for founders selling engagements above £50k who are tired of watching good prospects go quiet after the proposal.\\n\\nThat's you.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Open [INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME]\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS. When a prospect says 'your price is a bit high' — do you actually know what they mean?\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 3,\n    \"subject\": \"the £80k call I nearly didn't take\",\n    \"previewText\": \"I had the right service. The right prospect. And I still talked my way out of the deal in under 40 minutes.\",\n    \"body\": \"There was a point where I was winning work on reputation and losing it on calls.\\n\\nA referral would come in. Strong fit. Right budget. Warm introduction.\\n\\nAnd then I'd get on the call and explain everything.\\n\\nThe methodology. The process. The outcomes. The investment.\\n\\nI was thorough. I was credible. I was comprehensive.\\n\\nI was also, I later understood, terrifying to buy from.\\n\\nBecause I was doing all the talking — and none of the listening that makes a prospect feel understood.\\n\\nThe turning point was a call I almost didn't replay.\\n\\nA prospect had said \"we'll be in touch\" and vanished. I went back through the recording.\\n\\nI heard myself answer a question they never actually asked.\\n\\nI heard myself discount before they pushed back.\\n\\nI heard myself overexplain the price — which told them, without words, that I didn't believe it.\\n\\nThat recording broke something open.\\n\\nNot because I was doing it badly. Because I was doing it wrong — and there's a difference.\\n\\nI built [INSERT_HOST_NAME]'s entire approach around what I heard in that replay.\\n\\nMore on that Thursday.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply and tell me — have you ever replayed a lost call?\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS. The founder who closes at 40% does one thing differently in the first seven minutes of a discovery call. It's not what most people assume.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 5,\n    \"subject\": \"what happens when you stop defending the price\",\n    \"previewText\": \"The close rate doesn't move because of better objection-handling. It moves because of what happens before the objection exists.\",\n    \"body\": \"A fractional CFO came in with a consistent problem.\\n\\nDeals above £60k were stalling after the proposal. Prospects were engaged on the call. Then quiet.\\n\\nThe proposals were detailed, well-structured, clearly priced.\\n\\nThat was actually part of the problem.\\n\\nThe proposal was doing a job that should have been done on the discovery call — building the case for the investment.\\n\\nWhen a prospect reaches the proposal without already believing the price is right, the document becomes a negotiation, not a confirmation.\\n\\nOver [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION], long enough to see patterns across multiple deal cycles, the shift we worked on was structural.\\n\\nNot scripts. Not rebuttals. Not pricing tactics.\\n\\nThe change was earlier — in the specific questions asked in the first twenty minutes, and in the silence that followed them.\\n\\nWhen a prospect names the cost of their problem in their own words, they price-anchor themselves.\\n\\nYou don't need to defend £50k when they've just described a £300k problem.\\n\\nThe close rate moved. The discount conversations largely stopped.\\n\\nThe proposals got shorter, because they were confirming a decision that had already been made.\\n\\nIf you're still losing deals after strong discovery calls — the calls probably weren't as strong as they felt.\\n\\nThe next cohort of this programme has limited places.\\n\\nIf this is the right time, the link below is where to start.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Claim your place before the cohort closes\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS. Places in each cohort are limited by design — this work is 1:1, and [INSERT_HOST_NAME] keeps intake small. Once the cohort is full, the next entry point is weeks away.\"\n  }\n]"}
