[emailSequences] RETRY EXHAUST — FULL RAW CONTENT (from invokeLLM, post tool-use stringify): {"emails":"[\n  {\n    \"day\": 1,\n    \"subject\": \"The proposal wasn't the problem\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Something quieter killed the deal — and it happened before you ever sent anything.\",\n    \"body\": \"You sent a strong proposal.\\n\\nThe numbers were right. The scope was clear. You followed up.\\n\\nAnd then — nothing.\\n\\nNot a rejection. Not a negotiation. Just silence.\\n\\nHere's what most people do next: they rewrite the proposal. They lower the price. They add more deliverables to justify the number.\\n\\nNone of that fixes it.\\n\\nBecause the deal didn't die when they read the proposal. It died in the 90 seconds after you quoted the price — when something in your voice, your posture, or your phrasing signalled that you weren't sure you believed it either.\\n\\nProspects don't buy the service. They buy your certainty about the service.\\n\\nThis is what [INSERT_EVENT_NAME] is built around.\\n\\nOn [INSERT_EVENT_NAME], [INSERT_HOST_NAME] is going to show you exactly what's happening in those 90 seconds — and how to own them completely.\\n\\nThis is for women running high-value service businesses who are tired of watching good prospects go quiet after the proposal.\\n\\nIf that's you, you're going to want to be there live.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Add [INSERT_EVENT_NAME] to your calendar and show up live.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — [INSERT_HOST_NAME] is covering one specific pricing conversation technique on the day that won't be in any replay. More on that tomorrow.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 2,\n    \"subject\": \"She knew her number. She still froze.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Imagine you're sitting across from a prospect you've wanted for months — and the number you practiced feels suddenly wrong.\",\n    \"body\": \"Imagine you're a senior consultant. You've done this work for eight years. You know exactly what it's worth.\\n\\nYou've prepared. You've rehearsed the number. You even wrote it on a Post-it note inside your notebook.\\n\\nThe prospect leans forward and asks: \"So what does this look like investment-wise?\"\\n\\nAnd something happens.\\n\\nNot panic. Something quieter. A half-second hesitation. A softening in your voice. An invisible apology wrapped around the number before it even leaves your mouth.\\n\\n\"It's around… forty-five… but we can absolutely talk about what works for you.\"\\n\\nYou discounted before they pushed back. They hadn't even flinched.\\n\\nThat moment — that involuntary softening — is not a pricing problem. It's not a confidence problem in the way people usually mean it.\\n\\nIt's a disconnection between what you know your service is worth and what you actually believe you're allowed to charge for it.\\n\\nThose are two different things.\\n\\nAnd [INSERT_EVENT_NAME] is specifically designed to close that gap.\\n\\n[INSERT_HOST_NAME] will walk you through why this happens — and what to do instead.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Secure your spot at [INSERT_EVENT_NAME] and show up ready to change this.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The women who get the most from this event aren't the ones who struggle with pricing. They're the ones who are good at pricing and still feel something uncomfortable when they say the number out loud. If that's you — this is for you.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 3,\n    \"subject\": \"Confidence isn't the missing piece\",\n    \"previewText\": \"The thing you've been told to build more of is not what's stalling your sales. Here's what actually is.\",\n    \"body\": \"Every sales trainer will tell you the same thing: be more confident.\\n\\nSpeak with authority. Hold your price. Don't apologise.\\n\\nAnd you've heard it. You've practised it. You've stood in front of the mirror and said the number without flinching.\\n\\nBut on the call — something still shifts.\\n\\nHere's the counterintuitive truth: confidence is not the ingredient you're missing.\\n\\nThe women who discount under pressure, who overexplain their pricing, who watch good prospects go quiet after the proposal — they are not unconfident people. They are highly capable people operating inside a sales conversation they haven't yet made structurally safe.\\n\\nStructurally safe means: the conversation itself is built so that your price lands with weight, not apology — before your prospect has a chance to push back.\\n\\nMost people try to fix this by building more confidence. The actual fix is building a better conversation architecture.\\n\\nThat's the specific thing [INSERT_HOST_NAME] is teaching at [INSERT_EVENT_NAME].\\n\\nNot mindset. Not affirmations. A specific, repeatable structure for the sales conversation that makes discounting unnecessary.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Register for [INSERT_EVENT_NAME] and come prepared to rethink how your sales conversations are built.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — If you've ever left a sales call thinking 'I should have held my price' — that call was structurally wrong before you even started. [INSERT_HOST_NAME] will show you exactly where it broke down.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 4,\n    \"subject\": \"What nobody mentions about selling at this level\",\n    \"previewText\": \"It's not the close that costs you the deal. It's something that happens much earlier — and it's fixable in one conversation.\",\n    \"body\": \"Here's something most sales training completely skips.\\n\\nAt the level you're operating — £30k, £50k, £80k engagements — your prospects are not evaluating your service.\\n\\nThey decided they wanted to work with you before the proposal arrived.\\n\\nWhat they're evaluating in that final conversation is whether you are the kind of person who knows how to be paid well.\\n\\nThat is a completely different thing to evaluate. And it requires a completely different kind of presence.\\n\\nWhen you learn to show up in that conversation correctly — not confidently, correctly — something changes.\\n\\nProspects stop asking for discounts. Not because you've learned to say no better. Because the question stops arising.\\n\\nThat's the unexpected benefit of what [INSERT_HOST_NAME] teaches at [INSERT_EVENT_NAME].\\n\\nYou don't just close more deals. You stop having the painful conversations entirely. The \"we need to think about it\" calls. The \"can you do anything on the price\" emails. The proposals that go unanswered.\\n\\nThose conversations are a symptom. [INSERT_EVENT_NAME] treats the cause.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Join [INSERT_EVENT_NAME] live — this is the session that changes how your next sales conversation feels.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — There are limited spots for live attendance. Once they're gone, the replay won't include the live Q&A where [INSERT_HOST_NAME] works through real scenarios from the room.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 5,\n    \"subject\": \"Last chance before it's live\",\n    \"previewText\": \"After today, the conversation moves on without you — and the one thing you can't get from a replay starts in a few hours.\",\n    \"body\": \"[INSERT_EVENT_NAME] is live today.\\n\\nIf you're already registered — good. Show up. Be in the room. The people who attend live consistently get more out of this than those who catch the replay later.\\n\\nHere's why that matters today specifically.\\n\\n[INSERT_HOST_NAME] is running a live pricing conversation breakdown during the session. Real scenarios. Real responses. Real language you can use on your next call.\\n\\nThat part is not going into the replay.\\n\\nIf you're watching later, you'll get the framework. You won't get to watch [INSERT_HOST_NAME] work through the exact moment a prospect says \"that's a lot more than we expected\" — and show you precisely what to say next.\\n\\nThat exchange alone is worth showing up for.\\n\\nThe cohort is limited. Enrolment for the programme [INSERT_HOST_NAME] introduces today closes once those places fill — there is no waitlist and no second cohort in the near term.\\n\\nIf you've been sitting on the fence about attending — this is the moment to decide.\\n\\nGet in the room.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Join [INSERT_EVENT_NAME] live now — claim your seat before it starts.\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The women who enrol in the programme directly from today's session will receive access to a private follow-up session not available to anyone who joins later. That offer closes when the event ends.\"\n  }\n]"}
