[emailSequences] RETRY EXHAUST — FULL RAW CONTENT (from invokeLLM, post tool-use stringify): {"emails":"[\n  {\n    \"day\": 0,\n    \"subject\": \"Your download is waiting inside\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Plus: what I've noticed about the managers who actually make the delegation shift — and those who never do.\",\n    \"body\": \"Your copy of [INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME] is ready — grab it now using the button below.\\n\\nI'm [INSERT_HOST_NAME], and I've spent years working with first-time managers at tech companies on exactly one problem: you're doing your team's thinking for them.\\n\\nNot because you're a control freak.\\n\\nBecause nobody handed you a system that made letting go feel safe.\\n\\nOver the next three weeks, I'm going to share what actually works — the framework, the proof, and the specific moment most managers realise they've been the bottleneck all along.\\n\\nYou'll also hear from Rachel, Aiden, and Lin — three managers who were exactly where you are now.\\n\\nBut before any of that, here's a question I want you to sit with:\\n\\nHow many projects are sitting in your queue right now that only exist there because you haven't handed them off yet?\\n\\nI'll come back to that number in a couple of days.\\n\\nFor now — go read your download.\\n\\n[INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Download [INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME] now\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — In my next email, I'm going to share something I've noticed about that number you just counted. Most managers are surprised by what it actually reveals.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 2,\n    \"subject\": \"The number you counted yesterday\",\n    \"previewText\": \"It's not a workload problem. Here's what it actually tells you about where you're stuck.\",\n    \"body\": \"I've noticed something specific about first-time managers in tech who can't let go of the work.\\n\\nThe number of projects sitting with them isn't a capacity problem.\\n\\nIt's a trust problem — but not the kind you think.\\n\\nIt's not that you don't trust your team.\\n\\nIt's that you don't yet trust yourself to know when something has gone wrong fast enough to fix it.\\n\\nSo you stay close. You stay in the doc. You stay on the Slack thread.\\n\\nBecause if you're still touching it, you'll catch it.\\n\\n[INSERT_LEAD_MAGNET_NAME] gives you a starting framework. But here's what the download doesn't say:\\n\\nThe managers who make the delegation shift fastest are not the ones who get better at trusting their team first.\\n\\nThey're the ones who build a visibility system that lets them step back without going blind.\\n\\nThat's the actual unlock.\\n\\nDoes that land for you? Or does your situation feel different?\\n\\nHit reply and tell me — I read every response.\\n\\n[INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Hit reply and tell me what's actually stopping you\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — In a few days I'm going to name the exact moment in your week where the delegation problem shows up. Most managers don't realise it happens at the same time, every week, like clockwork.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 5,\n    \"subject\": \"It happens every Tuesday at 10am\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Not the big leadership crisis. The small one that drains you before lunch — and you've stopped noticing it.\",\n    \"body\": \"Here's where the delegation problem actually lives — not in the big strategic decisions.\\n\\nIt lives in the moment someone on your team sends a Slack message that says: \"Quick question — do you have five minutes?\"\\n\\nAnd you say yes. Every time.\\n\\nBecause saying no feels like abandoning them.\\n\\nAnd because if you're honest, part of you isn't sure they'll get it right without you.\\n\\nSo you hop on a call. You answer the question. You feel useful.\\n\\nAnd then you do it again an hour later. And again after lunch.\\n\\nBy the end of the day you haven't done the work only you can do — because you spent the day doing the work your team should own.\\n\\nThis isn't a time management problem.\\n\\nIt's a decision architecture problem. Your team has learned that the fastest path to an answer is you.\\n\\nSo they use it. Of course they do.\\n\\nDoes that feel like your week?\\n\\n[INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply with 'yes' if this is your Tuesday (or any other day)\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Next email I'll show you the framework that breaks this pattern. It's simpler than you think — and it has a name.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 8,\n    \"subject\": \"Why the delegation scripts fail\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Every manager tries the same fix. Here's what I figured out when it stopped working for the people I work with.\",\n    \"body\": \"I get asked this constantly: \"What do I actually say when I hand something off?\"\\n\\nThe question makes sense. But it's the wrong question.\\n\\nHere's what I spent years figuring out working with managers at tech companies:\\n\\nDelegation doesn't fail at the handoff. It fails two weeks later — when the project stalls and your team comes back to you because they never had genuine ownership.\\n\\nThe framework I use is called the Ownership Transfer Method.\\n\\nThe principle: authority and accountability have to move together. Hand off the task without handing off the decision rights — and you haven't delegated anything. You've just outsourced a to-do.\\n\\nThe proof: Aiden O'Connor, Marketing Manager at a Series B startup, went from 60-hour weeks to 45 within the cohort. His words: \"The delegation script alone changed how I run one-on-ones.\"\\n\\nIt's not about the words. It's about what the words transfer.\\n\\nWant me to walk you through how it works for the specific projects you can't let go of right now?\\n\\n[INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply with 'walk me through it' and I'll share the next step\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — In the next email I'm sharing what happened when one manager handed off a project she'd been holding for nine months. The outcome is not what you'd expect.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 12,\n    \"subject\": \"Nine months. One handoff. What happened.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Rachel was an Engineering Manager at a Series C SaaS company. She'd been holding the same project since the previous year.\",\n    \"body\": \"Rachel Park was an Engineering Manager at a Series C SaaS company.\\n\\nShe had one project she couldn't release. She'd been holding it for nine months.\\n\\nNot because she didn't want to hand it off. Because every time she thought about letting go, something would pull her back in — a missed deadline, a technical decision she didn't trust her team to make, a stakeholder who emailed her directly.\\n\\nBy week 8 of the cohort, she handed it off.\\n\\nHer words: \"By week 8 I had handed off the project I had been holding for nine months. My team is faster now.\"\\n\\nHere's what changed: Rachel didn't suddenly trust her team more.\\n\\nShe built a visibility structure that meant she didn't need to be inside the project to know it was on track.\\n\\nOnce she could see it from the outside, she could leave it there.\\n\\nLin Wei, Product Manager at a public company, described the same shift differently: \"I was the bottleneck on every launch. Three months later my team ships without me checking the doc.\"\\n\\nThe structure is learnable. It took both of them 12 weeks.\\n\\nIs the project you've been holding for too long coming to mind right now?\\n\\n[INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply and tell me which project you can't let go of\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Next email: the approach most first-time managers try before finding this one — and why it makes the delegation problem worse, not better.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 16,\n    \"subject\": \"The thing that makes it worse\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Most managers try this first. It feels logical. It quietly creates more bottlenecks.\",\n    \"body\": \"Most first-time managers try the same fix when they realise they're the bottleneck.\\n\\nThey document everything. SOPs, Notion wikis, Loom walkthroughs.\\n\\nIt makes sense. If the knowledge lives somewhere else, they can step back.\\n\\nHere's why it doesn't work: documentation captures what to do. It doesn't transfer ownership of why the decision matters — or the authority to make the call when something unexpected happens.\\n\\nSo the team reads the doc and still pings you. Because the doc didn't tell them what to do when it doesn't go to plan.\\n\\nYou haven't removed yourself from the loop. You've just added a step before they reach you.\\n\\nNow — the most common objection to the approach I use:\\n\\n\"My team isn't ready. If I hand this off, it'll break.\"\\n\\nHere's the honest answer: your team will make mistakes. That's not a risk — it's the mechanism. The question is whether you have a structure that catches mistakes early enough to fix them without you being inside every project.\\n\\nThat structure is exactly what the cohort builds.\\n\\nStill on the fence? What's the question I can answer?\\n\\n[INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply with your biggest concern — I'll give you a straight answer\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Next email is the only one where I'll talk about the cohort directly. I'll show you exactly what's included and what happens when the cohort starts.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 19,\n    \"subject\": \"Three projects. Twelve weeks. Here's the offer.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Cohort places are limited. This is the only email where I'll lay out exactly what's inside.\",\n    \"body\": \"Here's what the __REDTEAM___14_Cohort is:\\n\\nA [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION] group coaching programme for first-time managers at tech companies who are drowning in work they can't let go of.\\n\\nBy week 12, you hand off three projects you're currently holding. That's the specific, measurable outcome we build towards.\\n\\nHere's what's inside:\\n\\nThe Ownership Transfer Method — the framework Rachel, Aiden, and Lin all used. Weekly group coaching calls where we work on your actual projects, not hypotheticals. A delegation script that transfers authority, not just tasks. A visibility structure that lets you step back without going blind.\\n\\nAiden went from 60-hour weeks to 45 inside the cohort. Lin's team now ships without her checking the doc. Rachel handed off a project she'd held for nine months.\\n\\nThis is not a course you watch and forget. It's a live cohort with a small group — which means places are limited and close when they're gone.\\n\\nThe next cohort starts [INSERT_PROGRAMME_START_DATE].\\n\\n[INSERT_GUARANTEE_TERMS]\\n\\nIf you've been waiting to feel ready — this is the window.\\n\\n[INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now and claim your place before the cohort closes\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Cohort size is capped deliberately. When it's full, the next opportunity is the following cohort. If you're reading this, there are still places — but I can't tell you for how long.\"\n  }\n]"}
