[emailSequences] RETRY EXHAUST — FULL RAW CONTENT (from invokeLLM, post tool-use stringify): {"emails":"[\n  {\n    \"day\": -7,\n    \"subject\": \"I've been sitting on this for a while\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Something's almost ready. And it's specifically for the manager who can't stop doing everyone else's job.\",\n    \"body\": \"I've been working on something for a while now — and it's almost ready.\\n\\nIt's for the manager who's technically in charge but still the one answering every Slack message, reviewing every doc, and holding every project no one else seems to want to own.\\n\\nNot because you're a control freak.\\n\\nBecause nobody told you how to stop.\\n\\nThere's a specific moment most first-time managers hit. Your calendar looks like a game of Tetris you're losing. Your team is capable — you know that. But somehow everything still loops back to you.\\n\\nAnd the harder you work, the more the bottleneck tightens.\\n\\nWhat I've been building is designed to break that loop. Not with a new productivity system. Not with a delegation checklist you'll ignore by Thursday.\\n\\nSomething more structural than that.\\n\\nI'm not ready to share everything yet.\\n\\nBut I wanted you to be the first to know it's coming — because if you've ever said \"it's faster if I just do it myself,\" this is for you.\\n\\nWatch for my next email. I'll share the one thing most managers get completely backwards about delegation.\\n\\nIt might change how you see your entire week.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Watch for the next email — it arrives in a few days\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — If you forward this to a manager friend who's drowning right now, they'll thank you later. What's coming is genuinely useful.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": -4,\n    \"subject\": \"Delegation advice is backwards\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Everyone tells you to let go. That's the wrong instruction entirely — here's what actually works.\",\n    \"body\": \"Every piece of delegation advice tells you the same thing: let go.\\n\\nTrust your team. Step back. Give up control.\\n\\nThat advice is backwards.\\n\\nThe reason you can't delegate isn't a grip problem. It's a gap problem.\\n\\nYour team is waiting on you because somewhere between the handoff and the deadline, there's a gap — in context, in standards, or in confidence — that nobody has closed.\\n\\nAnd because you can feel that gap, you step back in to fill it.\\n\\nEvery time.\\n\\nSo the work comes back to you. Not because you're unwilling to let go. Because letting go without closing the gap just creates a mess you'll clean up later anyway.\\n\\nHere's what actually works: before you delegate the task, you delegate the judgment.\\n\\nYou build the conditions where your team can make the call you would make — without needing to ask you first.\\n\\nThat's not a soft skill. It's a specific process.\\n\\nAnd it's exactly what I've been building toward in what I'm about to share with you.\\n\\nI'll tell you more in two days.\\n\\nBut first — does this match your experience? Hit reply and tell me which project is currently stuck in your queue because you haven't been able to hand it off.\\n\\nI read every reply.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Hit reply — tell me which project you can't let go of right now\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — In the next email, I'll show you exactly what happened when a manager handed off a project she'd been holding for nine months. The result surprised even her.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": -2,\n    \"subject\": \"She'd held that project for 9 months\",\n    \"previewText\": \"By week 8 it was gone. Here's the structural shift that made it possible — and what it means for you.\",\n    \"body\": \"Rachel Park is an Engineering Manager at a Series C SaaS company.\\n\\nFor nine months, one project lived on her plate and nowhere else.\\n\\nShe knew the work. She'd started it. Every time she tried to hand it off, something pulled her back in — a question from the team, a decision that needed her, a standard that wasn't being met.\\n\\nSo she held it.\\n\\nBy week 8 of working through a structured delegation process, that project was gone.\\n\\nHer words: \"By week 8 I had handed off the project I had been holding for nine months. My team is faster now.\"\\n\\nNot because she finally learned to let go.\\n\\nBecause she closed the gaps first.\\n\\nShe built the conditions where her team could own the work — not just receive it.\\n\\nThat's the shift. From transferring tasks to transferring ownership.\\n\\nThose are not the same thing. Most delegation advice treats them as if they are.\\n\\nThe method I've been describing in these emails — the one that closes the gap before the handoff — is the foundation of what I'm opening in 48 hours.\\n\\nIt's a structured programme for first-time managers in tech who are still the bottleneck on their own team.\\n\\nDoors open [INSERT_CART_OPEN_DATE].\\n\\nI'll send you everything then — the name, what's included, the investment, and the cohort details.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Watch for the cart-open email on [INSERT_CART_OPEN_DATE]\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Aiden O'Connor went from 60-hour weeks to 45 inside the programme. Lin Wei's team now ships without her checking the doc. Both are real. Both started exactly where you are.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 0,\n    \"subject\": \"It's live — here's everything\",\n    \"previewText\": \"The programme, what's inside, the price, the guarantee — all of it, right here.\",\n    \"body\": \"Today, [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] is open for enrolment.\\n\\nHere's everything you need to know.\\n\\n[INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] is an [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION] coaching programme for first-time managers in tech — specifically built for the 0-to-18-month window when you're still the person your team waits on for every decision.\\n\\nBy the end of the programme, you will have handed off three projects you currently can't let go of.\\n\\nThat's the specific, measurable outcome we build toward.\\n\\nHere's what's included:\\n\\nWeekly 1:1 coaching sessions with [INSERT_HOST_NAME]. A step-by-step delegation framework built for tech environments. A repeatable handoff process your team can operate without you. Scripts for the conversations most managers avoid. A private cohort community for peer accountability. Access to all session recordings for the duration of the programme.\\n\\nThe investment: [INSERT_PRICE].\\n\\nThe guarantee: [INSERT_GUARANTEE_TERMS].\\n\\nThis cohort is limited to [INSERT_COHORT_LIMIT] managers. When those places are filled, enrolment closes.\\n\\nCart closes [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE] at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\\n\\nRachel Park handed off a nine-month-old project by week 8. Aiden O'Connor dropped from 60-hour weeks to 45. Lin Wei's team now ships without her checking the doc.\\n\\nThey all started exactly where you are right now.\\n\\nEnrol now and claim your place in this cohort.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now and claim your place in this cohort\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — [INSERT_COHORT_LIMIT] places. When they're gone, enrolment closes. No waitlist, no exceptions. If you're reading this, a spot still exists — but I can't promise that for long.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 1,\n    \"subject\": \"If you're the one Slack never sleeps on\",\n    \"previewText\": \"This is for the manager whose team is capable but somehow everything still lands back in your lap.\",\n    \"body\": \"If you're a first-time manager in a fast-moving tech team, here's what your week probably looks like.\\n\\nYour calendar is stacked. Your team is technically capable. But by 4pm every day, there are four messages waiting for a decision only you can apparently make.\\n\\nYou say yes to reviewing the doc. Again.\\n\\nYou jump into the thread. Again.\\n\\nYou tell yourself this is temporary — once the quarter settles, once the new hire ramps, once you get ahead of the backlog.\\n\\nBut the backlog doesn't shrink. It just changes shape.\\n\\nThis version of the problem isn't about effort. You're already working harder than anyone on your team.\\n\\nIt's about architecture.\\n\\nRight now, your team is set up to need you at every checkpoint. [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] restructures that — checkpoint by checkpoint, project by project — until your team can own the work without looping you in.\\n\\nLin Wei described her before: \"I was the bottleneck on every launch.\"\\n\\nHer after: \"Three months later my team ships without me checking the doc.\"\\n\\nSame team. Different architecture.\\n\\nIf that's the version of your job you want — where your team moves and you lead instead of execute — [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] is built for that exact transition.\\n\\nJoin the programme and claim your place now.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Join the programme and claim your place now\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Cart closes [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE] at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME]. If you're on the fence, the next email addresses the objection I hear most. It arrives in two days.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 3,\n    \"subject\": \"\\\"My team isn't ready to own it\\\"\",\n    \"previewText\": \"That's the real objection. And it deserves a real answer — not a pep talk.\",\n    \"body\": \"Here's what I hear most often at this point: \"My team isn't ready to own the work yet.\"\\n\\nNot \"I'm too busy to enrol.\" Not \"the timing is off.\"\\n\\nThis. Your team isn't ready.\\n\\nAnd you're probably right.\\n\\nBut here's what that sentence actually means: your team hasn't been set up to be ready. There's a difference.\\n\\nRight now, your team gets tasks. They don't get the judgment to go with the task — the context, the standards, the decision-making authority that would let them own the outcome instead of just execute a step.\\n\\nThat gap isn't your team's failure. It's a structural gap. And it's fixable.\\n\\nAiden O'Connor said the same thing before he started. By the end of the programme, he had gone from 60-hour weeks to 45 — not because his team changed, but because the handoff process changed.\\n\\nHis words: \"The delegation script alone changed how I run things.\"\\n\\nSame team. Better architecture.\\n\\nNow: if your team is genuinely new, genuinely undertrained, or genuinely under-resourced — that is a real constraint, and no programme fixes that. This isn't for a manager whose team can't do the work.\\n\\nIt's for a manager whose team can — but hasn't been given the conditions to prove it.\\n\\nIf that's you, enrol now before the cohort closes.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now before the cohort closes\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — [INSERT_GUARANTEE_TERMS]. You're not risking the investment. You're risking the next quarter of 60-hour weeks if you don't act.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 5,\n    \"subject\": \"I almost forgot to mention this\",\n    \"previewText\": \"There's a bonus I haven't talked about yet — and it's the one most managers say they needed on day one.\",\n    \"body\": \"I should have mentioned this when the cart opened.\\n\\nEvery manager who joins [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] before [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE] also gets access to the First-Time Manager Delegation Vault — a bonus resource library I'm including at [INSERT_BONUS_VALUE] in value.\\n\\nHere's what's inside:\\n\\nDone-for-you handoff templates you can adapt and send this week. Word-for-word scripts for the three conversations managers avoid most — pushing back on scope creep, telling your team a standard wasn't met, and handing off a project you started yourself. A video walkthrough of the gap-closing method I described in the pre-launch emails, mapped to the most common tech team structures.\\n\\nThis isn't bonus filler.\\n\\nIt's the toolkit most managers say they needed before their first week in the role — not after nine months of figuring it out alone.\\n\\nThe Vault is only available to managers who enrol before [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE] at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\\n\\nAfter that, the cart closes and the Vault closes with it.\\n\\nThe full package — [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION] of 1:1 coaching, the delegation framework, the cohort community, and the Vault — is [INSERT_PRICE].\\n\\nIf you've been on the fence, this is the moment to decide.\\n\\nEnrol now and unlock the full package.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now and unlock the full package including the Vault\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The Vault disappears when the cart closes. The programme continues, but this bonus does not carry forward to a future cohort. Enrol before [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE] to lock it in.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 7,\n    \"subject\": \"Closes tonight — what you lose access to\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Cart closes at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME] on [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE]. Here's exactly what shuts down when that happens.\",\n    \"body\": \"Cart closes [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_DATE] at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\\n\\nWhen it does, here's what you lose access to:\\n\\n[INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] — the [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION] coaching programme built to get you out of the bottleneck role and into actual leadership.\\n\\nThe First-Time Manager Delegation Vault — the done-for-you templates, scripts, and walkthroughs worth [INSERT_BONUS_VALUE] that close with the cart.\\n\\nThis cohort — limited to [INSERT_COHORT_LIMIT] managers. Once those places are filled, enrolment closes regardless of the cart-close date.\\n\\nRachel Park handed off a nine-month project by week 8. Lin Wei's team ships without her checking the doc. Aiden O'Connor reclaimed 15 hours a week inside the programme.\\n\\nThose results didn't come from working harder.\\n\\nThey came from changing the architecture of how their teams work.\\n\\nIf you're still reading this, you haven't decided yet.\\n\\nHere's the one question worth asking yourself: what does the next quarter look like if nothing changes?\\n\\nSame Slack messages. Same late nights. Same projects that only move when you push them.\\n\\nThe cart closes at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\\n\\nEnrol now before the cohort fills.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now before the cart closes tonight\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — [INSERT_COHORT_CLOSE_DATE] is the hard stop for cohort enrolment. Cart-close at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME] tonight is the first deadline — and it may be the only one if spots fill before then.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 7,\n    \"subject\": \"Last email. Make the call.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"You've been wondering if your team is actually the problem. Here's the honest answer before the cart shuts.\",\n    \"body\": \"You've been wondering the same thing since the first email.\\n\\nIs my team actually the problem — or is it me?\\n\\nHere's the honest answer: it's neither.\\n\\nIt's the gap between what your team was given and what they'd need to own the work without you.\\n\\nThat gap is fixable. [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] is the process for fixing it.\\n\\nBut the cart closes today at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\\n\\nAfter that, this cohort closes. The Delegation Vault closes. The [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION] 1:1 programme closes.\\n\\nHere's the choice as it actually stands.\\n\\nYou enrol today: in [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION], you've handed off three projects you're currently holding. Your team is faster. You are doing the job of a manager, not the job of your whole team.\\n\\nYou don't enrol today: next quarter looks like this one. The projects stay on your plate. The Slack messages keep landing. The 60-hour weeks don't move.\\n\\nThat's not pressure. That's just what's true.\\n\\nThis is the last email I'll send about [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] for this cohort.\\n\\nIf you're ready, enrol now before [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME].\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol now — cart closes at [INSERT_CART_CLOSE_TIME] today\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — [INSERT_GUARANTEE_TERMS]. The risk is not in enrolling. The risk is another quarter exactly like this one.\"\n  }\n]"}
[emailSequences] RETRY EXHAUST — FULL PARSED SNAPSHOT: {"emails":"[\n  {\n    \"day\": -7,\n    \"subject\": \"I've been sitting on this for a while\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Something's almost ready. And it's specifically for the manager who can't stop doing everyone else's job.\",\n    \"body\": \"I've been working on something for a while now — and it's almost ready.\\n\\nIt's for the manager who's technically in charge but still the one answering every Slack message, reviewing every doc, and holding every project no one else seems to want to own.\\n\\nNot because you're a control freak.\\n\\nBecause nobody told you how to stop.\\n\\nThere's a specific moment most first-time managers hit. Your calendar looks like a game of Tetris you're losing. Your team is capable — you know that. But somehow everything still loops back to you.\\n\\nAnd the harder you work, the more the bottleneck tightens.\\n\\nWhat I've been building is designed to break that loop. Not with a new productivity system. Not with a delegation checklist you'll ignore by Thursday.\\n\\nSomething more structural than that.\\n\\nI'm not ready to share everything yet.\\n\\nBut I wanted you to be the first to know it's coming — because if you've ever said \"it's faster if I just do it myself,\" this is for you.\\n\\nWatch for my next email. I'll share the one thing most managers get completely backwards about delegation.\\n\\nIt might change how you see your entire week.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Watch for the next email — it arrives in a few days\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — If you forward this to a manager friend who's drowning right now, they'll thank you later. What's coming is genuinely useful.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": -4,\n    \"subject\": \"Delegation advice is backwards\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Everyone tells you to let go. That's the wrong instruction entirely — here's what actually works.\",\n    \"body\": \"Every piece of delegation advice tells you the same thing: let go.\\n\\nTrust your team. Step back. Give up control.\\n\\nThat advice is backwards.\\n\\nThe reason you can't delegate isn't a grip problem. It's a gap problem.\\n\\nYour team is waiting on you because somewhere between the handoff and the deadline, there's a gap — in context, in standards, or in confidence — that nobody has closed.\\n\\nAnd because you can feel that gap, you step back in to fill it.\\n\\nEvery time.\\n\\nSo the work comes back to you. Not because you're unwilling to let go. Because letting go without closing the gap just creates a mess you'll clean up later anyway.\\n\\nHere's what actually works: before you delegate the task, you delegate the judgment.\\n\\nYou build the conditions where your team can make the call you would make — without needing to ask you first.\\n\\nThat's not a soft skill. It's a specific process.\\n\\nAnd it's exactly what I've been building toward in what I'm about to share with you.\\n\\nI'll tell you more in two days.\\n\\nBut first — does this match your experience? Hit reply and tell me which project is currently stuck in your queue because you haven't been able to hand it off.\\n\\nI read every reply.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Hit reply — tell me which project you can't let go of right now\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — In the next email, I'll show you exactly what happened when a manager handed off a project she'd been holding for nine months. The result surprised even her.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": -2,\n    \"subject\": \"She'd held that project for 9 months\",\n    \"previewText\": \"By week 8 it was gone. Here's the structural shift that made it possible — and what it means for you.\",\n    \"body\": \"Rachel Park is an Engineering Manager at a Series C SaaS company.\\n\\nFor nine months, one project lived on her plate and nowhere else.\\n\\nShe knew the work. She'd started it. Every time she tried to hand it off, something pulled her back in — a question from the team, a decision that needed her, a standard that wasn't being met.\\n\\nSo she held it.\\n\\nBy week 8 of working through a structured delegation process, that project was gone.\\n\\nHer words: \"By week 8 I had handed off the project I had been holding for nine months. My team is faster now.\"\\n\\nNot because she finally learned to let go.\\n\\nBecause she closed the gaps first.\\n\\nShe built the conditions where her team could own the work — not just receive it.\\n\\nThat's the shift. From transferring tasks to transferring ownership.\\n\\nThose are not the same thing. Most delegation advice treats them as if they are.\\n\\nThe method I've been describing in these emails — the one that closes the gap before the handoff — is the foundation of what I'm opening in 48 hours.\\n\\nIt's a structured programme for first-time managers in tech who are still the bottleneck on their own team.\\n\\nDoors open [INSERT_CART_OPEN_DATE].\\n\\nI'll send you everything then — the name, what's included, the investment, and the cohort details.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Watch for the cart-open email on [INSERT_CART_OPEN_DATE]\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Aiden O'Connor went from 60-hour weeks to 45 inside the programme. Lin Wei's team now ships without her checking the doc. Both are real. Both started exactly where you are.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 0,\n    \"subject\": \"It's live — here's everything\",\n    \"previewText\": \"The programme, what's inside, the price, the guarantee — all of it, right here.\",\n    \"body\": \"Today, [INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] is open for enrolment.\\n\\nHere's everything you need to know.\\n\\n[INSERT_LAUNCH_PRODUCT_NAME] is an [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION] coaching programme for first-time managers in tech — specifically built for the 0-to-18-month window when you're still the person your team waits on for every decision.\\n\\nBy the end of the programme, you will have handed off three projects you currently can't let go of.\\n\\nThat's the specific, measurable outcome we bui