uld nod through the demo. Then ask for \"time to evaluate.\"\\n\\nThen go quiet.\\n\\nOne day a friendly VP sent back the sales deck with three words in the subject line: \"What do you do?\"\\n\\n[INSERT_HOST_NAME] had spent eighteen months building a product and about four hours explaining it.\\n\\nThe deck was full of features. The homepage was full of capabilities. The pricing page was full of tiers.\\n\\nNone of them answered the only question that mattered: why this, for us, right now?\\n\\nThe turning point wasn't a new product. It wasn't a rebrand.\\n\\nIt was rebuilding the positioning from the outside in — starting with what a sceptical buyer needed to believe before they could say yes, and working backwards.\\n\\nOnce that was clear, everything else got simpler.\\n\\nSales cycles shortened. Objections changed character. Deals started closing at the first meeting.\\n\\nIf you've been watching good prospects go quiet after demos, it's probably not your product.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Reply and tell me where your deals are stalling\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS. Tomorrow I'm sharing a specific founder story — a before and after with numbers — that might explain exactly why your pricing page is creating confusion instead of confidence.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 5,\n    \"subject\": \"1.8% to 4.6%. Same product.\",\n    \"previewText\": \"No redesign. No new features. One positioning change. Here's exactly what shifted.\",\n    \"body\": \"A Series B founder running a vertical SaaS came in with a homepage converting at 1.8%.\\n\\nNot because the product was weak — it was genuinely strong in its category.\\n\\nThe problem was her homepage was written for someone who already understood the category.\\n\\nMost visitors didn't. They landed, scanned, felt uncertain, and left.\\n\\nHer pricing page made it worse. Three tiers with feature lists that assumed the visitor already knew which problem they were solving.\\n\\nHer sales deck repeated the same assumptions in slide form.\\n\\nThree surfaces. Three versions of the same confusion.\\n\\nOver [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION], we rebuilt the positioning doc first — the single source of truth for who this product is for, what it replaces, and why now.\\n\\nThen the homepage. Then the deck.\\n\\nEight weeks after launch, homepage conversion hit 4.6%.\\n\\nShe didn't change the product. She changed what the product said about itself — and who it said it to.\\n\\nYuki Tanaka said it plainly: \"Our homepage conversion went from 1.8% to 4.6% in eight weeks after the rewrite.\"\\n\\nPedro Alves, co-founder of a dev-tools startup, put it this way: \"We had spent two years confusing prospects with feature-first messaging. The positioning doc untangled it.\"\\n\\nIf your three surfaces are each saying something slightly different, there's a straightforward fix.\\n\\nAnd it starts with one conversation.\\n\\n— [INSERT_HOST_NAME]\",\n    \"cta\": \"Claim your free consultation\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS. There are a limited number of places in the current cohort — and enrolment closes [INSERT_CART_CLOSE]. If your positioning has drifted, this is the window to fix all three surfaces at once.\"\n  }\n]"}
