[emailSequences] RETRY EXHAUST — FULL PARSED SNAPSHOT: {"emails":"[\n  {\n    \"day\": 1,\n    \"subject\": \"The sentence you couldn't finish\",\n    \"previewText\": \"You started to answer the question. Then you stopped. That stop is the most important data point you have right now.\",\n    \"body\": \"You already know something shifted at [INSERT_EVENT_NAME].\\n\\nNot a tactic. Not a framework. Something quieter and harder to name.\\n\\nHere is the one thing I want you to sit with today.\\n\\nAt some point during the event, you probably heard a description of a woman who has built everything correctly — and still cannot answer a simple question about what she actually wants.\\n\\nIf that landed differently than the rest of what you heard, it was not a coincidence.\\n\\nThat moment of recognition is not a problem to solve. It is a door.\\n\\nEvery week that door stays open without you walking through it is another week of making decisions on autopilot — outsourcing the answer to logic, obligation, or what seems like the correct next step.\\n\\nYou have been doing that for a long time. You are very good at it. And it is costing you something you cannot invoice for.\\n\\n[INSERT_OFFER_NAME] is the structured process for what comes next.\\n\\nNot therapy. Not another framework. A forensic, 1:1 process for distinguishing who you actually are from the roles you have been performing — so the next decision you make comes from you, not from the script.\\n\\nIf that is the conversation you are ready to have, the next step is below.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Learn how [INSERT_OFFER_NAME] works — and claim your place: [INSERT_OFFER_LINK]\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — There are [INSERT_COHORT_LIMIT] places in this cohort. If you are reading this on the day it arrived, some are already gone.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 2,\n    \"subject\": \"She stopped mid-sentence too\",\n    \"previewText\": \"A senior commercial leader. A question she couldn't answer. Here's what the forensic process found — and what shifted in 8 weeks.\",\n    \"body\": \"Here is a real starting point.\\n\\nA senior commercial leader — 15 years of high performance, consistent promotion, respected by her organisation — was asked a simple question by someone close to her.\\n\\nWhat do you actually want?\\n\\nShe started to answer with the strategic version. The sensible next move. The plan.\\n\\nAnd stopped.\\n\\nNot because she was emotional. Because she genuinely did not know.\\n\\nShe had spent so long adapting to what each room required from her that she could no longer tell a genuine preference from a conditioned one.\\n\\nThat is not a therapy problem. There was nothing to heal. The identity she was living inside had been built deliberately, in response to real incentives, and it worked — by every external measure.\\n\\nWhat she needed was not a framework to layer on top. She needed a forensic process to work backwards through the specific roles, relationships, and survival strategies that had built over her original self.\\n\\nBy week eight of [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION], she could sit in a room full of people who needed something from her — a board, a partner, a direct report — without automatically becoming what that room required.\\n\\nShe described it as the first time in over a decade she had spoken from a stable place rather than from anxiety about how she would be received.\\n\\nAnders Bjornsson, a CEO who worked through a related process with [INSERT_HOST_NAME], put it simply: *\"The shift cut our sales cycle from 90 days to 45.\"* External results follow internal clarity. Every time.\\n\\nIf her starting point sounds like your current address, the next step is below.\",\n    \"cta\": \"See how [INSERT_OFFER_NAME] is structured and reserve your place: [INSERT_OFFER_LINK]\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The women who get the most from this process are the ones who recognise the starting point, not just the outcome. If you recognised it, that matters.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 3,\n    \"subject\": \"You've already tried the obvious things\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Therapy didn't fix it. The Enneagram didn't fix it. That's not failure — it's evidence the problem is structural, not emotional.\",\n    \"body\": \"The real objection you have right now is not about price or timing.\\n\\nIt is this: *I have already tried to figure this out and I am still here.*\\n\\nTherapy. Coaching. StrengthsFinder. Human Design. The Enneagram. Possibly all five.\\n\\nAnd you are still standing in the same place — accomplished, respected, and unable to answer a basic question about what you actually want.\\n\\nHere is why none of it worked. And it is not because you did not do the work.\\n\\nTherapy is oriented around healing past wounds. This is not a wound. The identity you are living inside was built deliberately, in response to real incentives, and it functions extremely well by external measures. There is nothing to heal.\\n\\nPersonality frameworks — every single one — return a category. And you are already expert at adopting categories. You can read an Enneagram result and immediately know how to perform it. The problem was never that you lacked a label.\\n\\nThe problem is that you have spent so long adapting to contexts that you cannot reliably tell a genuine preference from a conditioned one.\\n\\n[INSERT_OFFER_NAME] does not begin with assessment. It begins with elimination — systematically removing the specific roles, relationships, and survival strategies that have layered over your original self, and identifying what remains consistently when those layers are gone.\\n\\nThis is a different process. Structurally different. And it is the reason women who arrive here after trying everything else describe it as the first thing that actually moved.\\n\\nEvery week you spend inside the wrong process is another week the real question stays unanswered.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Understand the structural difference — and claim your place in [INSERT_OFFER_NAME]: [INSERT_OFFER_LINK]\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Yuki Tanaka's homepage conversion went from 1.8% to 4.6% after working through a similar forensic clarity process. External results follow internal clarity. That is not a coincidence.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 4,\n    \"subject\": \"The thing nobody mentioned at the event\",\n    \"previewText\": \"There's one part of [INSERT_OFFER_NAME] that doesn't get announced upfront — and it's the piece most women say they needed most.\",\n    \"body\": \"You heard about the core process at [INSERT_EVENT_NAME].\\n\\nWhat nobody mentioned — because it is only revealed to enrolled members — is what you walk away holding at the end.\\n\\nEvery woman who completes [INSERT_OFFER_NAME] leaves with a written personal identity map.\\n\\nNot a vision board. Not a mission statement. Not an Enneagram type printed on a PDF.\\n\\nA specific, private document — built from the forensic material of your actual life — that distinguishes who you are from the roles you perform.\\n\\nThis is the piece that makes every decision after this programme different.\\n\\nBecause the reason major decisions have felt so hard is not that you lack information or intelligence. It is that you have been trying to make them without a stable internal reference point.\\n\\nYou have been outsourcing the answer to logic, obligation, or what the room seems to require.\\n\\nThe identity map changes that. Permanently.\\n\\nOn its own, a document like this — built through 1:1 forensic work with a practitioner who knows how to construct it — carries a value of [INSERT_PRICE] or more. It is not a bonus in the traditional sense. It is the deliverable that makes everything else in the programme stick.\\n\\nIt is included in [INSERT_OFFER_NAME] at no additional cost.\\n\\nThe total value of everything inside this programme sits well above [INSERT_PRICE]. The enrolment investment is [INSERT_PRICE].\\n\\nPedro Alves described the moment his positioning doc untangled two years of confusion in a single sitting. That is what a well-built identity document does. It does not add to what you know. It removes what was obscuring what you already knew.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Claim your place in [INSERT_OFFER_NAME] and receive your identity map: [INSERT_OFFER_LINK]\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — Enrolment closes on [INSERT_DEADLINE]. The identity map is built inside the programme — it cannot be purchased separately.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 5,\n    \"subject\": \"What if it doesn't work for you?\",\n    \"previewText\": \"That's the right question to ask. Here is the honest answer — and why keeping your money may cost you more than spending it.\",\n    \"body\": \"You should be asking this question before you enrol.\\n\\nWhat if I go through [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION] of this and come out the other side no clearer than when I started?\\n\\nHere is the honest answer.\\n\\n[INSERT_HOST_NAME] has worked with 31 verified clients. The average rating across 27 reviews is 4.95 out of 5. That is not a marketing number — it is the actual record.\\n\\nBut you are not 31 people. You are one specific woman with one specific biography. So the real risk question is not about the aggregate. It is about you.\\n\\nThe fit-check process before enrolment exists for exactly this reason. [INSERT_HOST_NAME] does not accept every application. If the process is not right for where you are, that conversation will surface it before you spend anything.\\n\\nThe guarantee terms are: [INSERT_GUARANTEE_TERMS].\\n\\nNow here is the question nobody asks.\\n\\nWhat does it cost to not do this?\\n\\nAnother year of making decisions from the script rather than from yourself. Another year of declining the right things and accepting the wrong ones because you cannot reliably tell the difference. Another year of mid-sentence stops when someone who loves you asks a simple question.\\n\\nThat cost does not appear on a bank statement. But it is compounding every month you stay in the same place.\\n\\nThe decision is not \\\"should I spend [INSERT_PRICE].\\\"\\n\\nThe decision is which risk is actually larger.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Review the full programme and the guarantee — then claim your place: [INSERT_OFFER_LINK]\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — The women who wait for certainty before enrolling are usually the ones who needed this most. Certainty is not the starting point. It is the outcome.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 6,\n    \"subject\": \"What closes on [INSERT_COHORT_CLOSE_DATE]\",\n    \"previewText\": \"It's not just the price. It's the specific cohort — and [INSERT_COHORT_LIMIT] places were always the ceiling.\",\n    \"body\": \"This is the practical email.\\n\\nEnrolment for this cohort of [INSERT_OFFER_NAME] closes on [INSERT_COHORT_CLOSE_DATE].\\n\\nThere are [INSERT_COHORT_LIMIT] places. That number is not a marketing mechanism — it is a function of how the 1:1 work is delivered. [INSERT_HOST_NAME] cannot do this work at the level it requires with more than [INSERT_COHORT_LIMIT] clients at once.\\n\\nWhen this cohort closes, the next opening is not guaranteed on any specific timeline.\\n\\nHere is what that means practically.\\n\\nIf you are waiting for a better moment — a quieter quarter, a resolved situation at work, a feeling of being ready — that moment is a story you are telling yourself to avoid the decision.\\n\\nThe women who attended [INSERT_EVENT_NAME] and enrolled within 48 hours did not have less going on than you. They simply recognised that waiting is also a choice, and that choice has a cost.\\n\\nEvery week you stay where you are is another week of making the important decisions from the wrong place — from obligation, from logic, from what the room seems to need.\\n\\nNot from yourself.\\n\\nYou have been doing that for a long time already.\\n\\nThe question is not whether you are ready. The question is whether you are done waiting to be.\\n\\n[INSERT_COHORT_LIMIT] places. Closes [INSERT_COHORT_CLOSE_DATE]. After that, the door closes on this cohort.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Claim one of the remaining places before [INSERT_COHORT_CLOSE_DATE]: [INSERT_OFFER_LINK]\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — If you are unsure whether this is the right fit, that conversation can happen before you enrol. But it has to happen before [INSERT_COHORT_CLOSE_DATE], not after.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 7,\n    \"subject\": \"Do you actually want this — or not?\",\n    \"previewText\": \"Not the strategic answer. Not the sensible next step. The actual answer. This is the last email. Here is what it comes down to.\",\n    \"body\": \"You have been wondering one thing since [INSERT_EVENT_NAME].\\n\\nNot whether the programme works. Not whether you can afford it.\\n\\nThis: *Am I the kind of person who actually does something about this — or the kind who recognises the problem and keeps going anyway?*\\n\\nHere is the answer.\\n\\nYou do not know yet. Neither do I. That is decided by what happens today.\\n\\nIf you enrol before [INSERT_DEADLINE]: In [INSERT_PROGRAMME_DURATION], you will have a written identity map built from the forensic material of your actual life. You will be able to answer the question your husband, your manager, or the honest part of yourself keeps asking. You will have stopped outsourcing the answer.\\n\\nIf you do not enrol today: You will return to the script. The strategic next step. The sensible plan. And six months from now, someone will ask you a simple question and you will start to answer — and stop mid-sentence again.\\n\\nBoth outcomes are available to you right now.\\n\\nThis is the last email in this sequence. The cohort closes at [INSERT_DEADLINE]. After that, this specific door closes.\\n\\n[INSERT_HOST_NAME] has a 4.95 rating from 27 reviews and 31 verified clients who made this decision. Every single one of them had reasons to wait.\\n\\nNone of them are sorry they didn't.\",\n    \"cta\": \"Enrol in [INSERT_OFFER_NAME] before [INSERT_DEADLINE]: [INSERT_OFFER_LINK]\",\n    \"ps\": \"PS — This is not a soft